\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{A connection background and its positive precision}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Fix a finite or compact Hausdorff group #{G} and a finite-dimensional real or
complex representation #{\rho:G\to\operatorname{GL}(F)}, continuous
in the compact case. Supply a positive inner product for which #{\rho}
is orthogonal or unitary. If necessary, start from any positive inner
product and average it:
##{\langle x,y\rangle_G
=\int_G\langle\rho(g)x,\rho(g)y\rangle_0\,dg.}
Here #{dg} is normalized right Haar measure; for a finite group it is the
uniform average. Haar existence and inversion from left to right are
given in \citet{Theorem 4.3, pp. 6–9; Proposition 4.1, pp. 4–5}{gleason2010haar}.
Compactness makes its nonzero total mass finite, allowing normalization.
Right invariance gives invariance of the displayed
pairing. The integrand is continuous and strictly positive for
#{x=y\ne0}, so the average is positive. The invariant metric is part
of the data used below.}

\p{On a [finite incidence presentation](fdip-0002), a
\newvocab{connection} assigns #{U_e\in G} to every chosen oriented
edge. Reversing that edge assigns #{U_{\bar e}=U_e^{-1}}.
Set #{H=F^V} and #{K=F^E}, with the sums of the supplied fiber inner
products. Write #{U_e} also for #{\rho(U_e)} in operator formulas.
For scalar weights #{w_e>0} and mass #{m>0}, let #{W} act on
edge fields by #{(Wa)(e)=w_ea(e)}. Define
##{\begin{aligned}
(\extd_Uf)(e)&=f(h(e))-U_ef(s(e)),\\
L_U&=\extd_U^\dagger W\extd_U,\qquad Q_U=L_U+m^2I_H.
\end{aligned}}
In a basis of the fiber, this weight is the block-diagonal operator
of \ref{fdip-00LT}.
The adjoint uses these declared inner products. Directly,
##{\langle f,Q_Uf\rangle
=\sum_e w_e\|f(h(e))-U_ef(s(e))\|^2+m^2\|f\|^2.}
Thus #{Q_U\ge m^2I_H>0}. This is the unitary connection version of
the [difference energy](fdip-0014); the adjoint qualification is also
explicit in \citet{Section 3.3, p. 6}{kenyon2011spanning}.}

\p{At #{m=0}, the same sum shows
##{\ker L_U=\ker\extd_U
=\{f:f(h(e))=U_ef(s(e))\text{ for every }e\}.}
Its elements are called \newvocab{parallel sections}. The unpinned
massless Gaussian is normalizable exactly when this space is zero:
then #{L_U} is positive definite; otherwise the density is constant
along a nonzero kernel direction and its integral diverges. This is
separate from the [pinned construction](fdip-003V). Nontrivial group
holonomy need not eliminate all parallel vectors in a higher-dimensional
or nonfaithful representation.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.ker_adjoint_comp_self} gives
#{\ker(A^*A)=\ker A} for finite-dimensional inner-product spaces.
Apply it to #{A=W^{1/2}\extd_U}; positive weights make #{W^{1/2}}
invertible. This supports the kernel identity, not the connection,
holonomy or normalizability assertions.}
